# [[.Name]]

A Goa / Goa-AI service created by `highbay init` or the `init_project` MCP tool.
It is a blank starting point: the service has no methods yet, and its test
suite only checks that the program starts. Each change you agree to in Highbay
adds behavior and the tests that prove it.

## What is here

- `design/` declares the `[[.Name]]` service (`design/design.go`), its C4
  architecture model (`design/model.go`) and its Story (`design/story.go`):
  who uses the service and the flows their requests take. The service has no
  methods and the Story has no flows yet.
- `gen/` is generated from `design/`. The guide edits the next design in its
  draft and proposes a revision for you to adopt. The builder calls the
  `generate_code` MCP tool on the Highbay project server to write that agreed
  design into its working copy's `design/` and regenerate `gen/`; it edits
  neither directory. An operator can run `highbay gen` in this directory: it
  runs Goa's generator for this module with the module's own versions. Never
  edit `gen/`. Once the design declares an agent, generation also writes
  `AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md` here for coding agents; commit it with `gen/`.
- `cmd/[[.Name]]` serves the API on the address given with `-listen host:port`
  and prints the address once it has bound it. Nothing is mounted on it yet.
- `eval/` is a separate Go module: the evaluator suite. Its one scenario,
  `starts_and_listens`, starts the `[[.Name]]` binary and checks that it
  binds an address and accepts connections on it. Its program, `eval/gen/cmd/[[.Name]]-evals`, is
  generated with the rest of `eval/gen/` from `eval/design/` and calls
  `eval/acceptance`: its `Name`, `Hooks`, `New`, `Live` and
  `LiveThroughGateway`. `Live` and `LiveThroughGateway` refuse until a change
  adds an agent, because a live copy exists to try prompt wording and this
  service calls no AI model yet. `eval/design/story_tests.go` lists the tests
  the Story requires, one scenario per finished flow; Highbay writes it again
  from the agreed Story during a build, so leave it as it is.

The evaluator suite lives in this repository, so anyone who can read the code
can read its scenarios; none of them is hidden. Keep scenarios that must stay
hidden from builders in an evaluator repository they cannot read.

## Next steps

If you created this project with `highbay init`, make the first commit and start
Highbay in this directory:

```sh
git init
git add .gitignore README.md go.mod go.sum design gen cmd eval
git commit -m "Start [[.Name]] from highbay init"
highbay
```

`highbay` starts this project's server, or attaches to it when it is already
running, and opens the project's views page in your browser. If the guide
created this project with `init_project`, the Highbay project server has already
initialized the Git repository, created the initial commit and bound itself to
this directory.

For each change:

1. You describe what you want to your coding agent, the guide. It drafts the
   design and the Story and proposes a Change Card, which you confirm on the
   views page.
2. You start the build there. The builder writes the service in its own
   working copy and, without stored verification settings, a test writer
   writes the tests the Story requires in `eval/`.
3. The builder checks its uncommitted files with `check_project`, commits on
   its own branch and calls `verify_change`.
4. When a verification passes, you review its result and approve it on the
   views page.

See the
[MCP tools reference](https://github.com/goadesign/highbay/blob/main/docs/REFERENCE.md#mcp-tools)
for each tool's inputs and results. Coding agents read the architecture and
evaluator guide at `highbay://golden-path`.

`highbay verify` and `highbay approve` are operator tools that check a commit
outside the server. To verify the current commit against the evaluator suite
yourself:

```sh
VERIFY_DIR="$(mktemp -d)"
WORK_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/work"
REPORT_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/reports"
highbay verify \
  -candidate-repo . -candidate-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -binary [[.Name]]=./cmd/[[.Name]] \
  -suite-repo . -suite-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -suite-module eval -suite-command ./gen/cmd/[[.Name]]-evals \
  -request-revision starter -work-dir "$WORK_DIR" -report-dir "$REPORT_DIR" -builder-view
```

`highbay verify` keeps `$WORK_DIR` after the run: it holds the clones, the
built binaries, and the evaluator's private evidence. Do not share
`$WORK_DIR`, `$REPORT_DIR`, or setup and execution failure output with coding
agents that build the service.
